1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:01,040
Welcome back!

2
00:00:01,820 --> 00:00:05,520
Today we start Level Five, the spelling rules.

3
00:00:06,700 --> 00:00:11,440
There are six rules in this level, and I'm going to show you all of them right now.

4
00:00:13,120 --> 00:00:14,420
This one is called Floss.

5
00:00:15,240 --> 00:00:17,620
It doubles letters at the end of a word.

6
00:00:19,300 --> 00:00:21,440
This one is the C-K rule.

7
00:00:21,860 --> 00:00:25,040
It helps us choose the right spelling for the /k/ sound.

8
00:00:26,840 --> 00:00:29,240
Then N-G and N-K.

9
00:00:30,100 --> 00:00:32,620
Those two letters team up to make new sounds.

10
00:00:34,600 --> 00:00:37,860
Then X, one letter hiding two sounds.

11
00:00:39,820 --> 00:00:41,920
And last, the tricky W.

12
00:00:42,480 --> 00:00:45,000
It can change the sound of the letter next to it.

13
00:00:47,000 --> 00:00:47,160
Six rules.

14
00:00:48,500 --> 00:00:51,080
Every single one is about a short vowel.

15
00:00:51,780 --> 00:00:54,210
Today we'll learn the first two properly...

16
00:00:54,900 --> 00:00:57,240
Floss and C-K.

17
00:00:57,280 --> 00:00:59,420
The other four are waiting for you in Part Two.

18
00:01:01,560 --> 00:01:02,520
Let's start with Floss.

19
00:01:04,620 --> 00:01:05,920
Look at these four words.

20
00:01:06,940 --> 00:01:07,220
Off.

21
00:01:08,500 --> 00:01:08,900
Bell.

22
00:01:09,840 --> 00:01:10,380
Miss.

23
00:01:11,840 --> 00:01:12,360
Buzz.

24
00:01:13,620 --> 00:01:16,010
Now look carefully at the end of each word.

25
00:01:16,880 --> 00:01:17,440
Two Fs.

26
00:01:18,740 --> 00:01:19,460
Two Ls.

27
00:01:20,600 --> 00:01:21,460
Two Ss.

28
00:01:22,660 --> 00:01:23,440
Two Zs.

29
00:01:24,520 --> 00:01:26,740
Every one ends with a doubled letter.

30
00:01:27,660 --> 00:01:29,200
So why does English do that?

31
00:01:30,180 --> 00:01:31,050
There's a reason...

32
00:01:31,800 --> 00:01:32,920
and it's a good one.

33
00:01:34,600 --> 00:01:38,380
The rule has three parts, and all three must be true.

34
00:01:39,420 --> 00:01:41,930
First, the word must have one syllable.

35
00:01:42,600 --> 00:01:44,120
That means one clap.

36
00:01:45,200 --> 00:01:45,840
Clap with me.

37
00:01:46,680 --> 00:01:47,020
Off...

38
00:01:48,580 --> 00:01:49,100
Bell...

39
00:01:50,200 --> 00:01:50,800
Miss...

40
00:01:52,160 --> 00:01:52,800
Buzz.

41
00:01:53,820 --> 00:01:55,360
One clap every time.

42
00:01:56,520 --> 00:01:58,860
Second, the vowel must be short.

43
00:01:59,900 --> 00:02:01,340
Listen.

44
00:02:01,340 --> 00:02:02,260
Off...

45
00:02:02,340 --> 00:02:04,180
short o.

46
00:02:04,260 --> 00:02:05,300
Bell...

47
00:02:05,340 --> 00:02:07,320
short e.

48
00:02:07,380 --> 00:02:07,620
Miss...

49
00:02:08,420 --> 00:02:10,540
short i.

50
00:02:10,560 --> 00:02:11,040
Buzz...

51
00:02:11,600 --> 00:02:12,480
short u.

52
00:02:13,700 --> 00:02:19,140
Third, the word must end with F, L, S or Z.

53
00:02:20,120 --> 00:02:21,600
Only those four letters.

54
00:02:22,780 --> 00:02:24,580
When all three parts are true...

55
00:02:25,240 --> 00:02:26,500
double the last letter.

56
00:02:28,360 --> 00:02:29,750
Let's try some more together.

57
00:02:31,280 --> 00:02:36,080
Cliff ends with F, so it takes two Fs.

58
00:02:36,280 --> 00:02:39,960
Tall ends with L, so it takes two Ls.

59
00:02:41,660 --> 00:02:45,560
Grass ends with S, so it takes two Ss.

60
00:02:47,240 --> 00:02:51,020
Fizz ends with Z, so it takes two Zs.

61
00:02:52,980 --> 00:02:54,180
Shell.

62
00:02:54,200 --> 00:02:54,560
Dress.

63
00:02:55,600 --> 00:02:58,640
Both are doubled, and both follow the same rule.

64
00:03:00,240 --> 00:03:01,670
You're picking this up quickly.

65
00:03:02,000 --> 00:03:02,840
Well done!

66
00:03:04,460 --> 00:03:05,950
Now here's something interesting.

67
00:03:06,920 --> 00:03:08,140
Listen to these sounds.

68
00:03:09,500 --> 00:03:11,300
Fffff...

69
00:03:11,300 --> 00:03:11,500
Lllll...

70
00:03:12,660 --> 00:03:14,120
Sssss...

71
00:03:14,120 --> 00:03:14,980
Zzzzz...

72
00:03:16,360 --> 00:03:17,090
Did you notice?

73
00:03:18,160 --> 00:03:20,740
You can stretch every one of those sounds.

74
00:03:21,560 --> 00:03:24,000
The doubled letter gives your brain a clue.

75
00:03:24,460 --> 00:03:27,320
It says, "The vowel before me is short."

76
00:03:28,720 --> 00:03:32,120
Without that clue, you might try a long vowel instead.

77
00:03:32,940 --> 00:03:33,880
So remember this.

78
00:03:34,960 --> 00:03:35,740
Short vowel...

79
00:03:36,440 --> 00:03:37,240
double letter.

80
00:03:37,900 --> 00:03:39,040
They belong together.

81
00:03:40,860 --> 00:03:42,500
Now let's look at the other side.

82
00:03:43,600 --> 00:03:43,780
Leaf.

83
00:03:44,960 --> 00:03:45,520
Feel.

84
00:03:46,560 --> 00:03:47,100
Tool.

85
00:03:48,180 --> 00:03:48,760
Pool.

86
00:03:49,980 --> 00:03:50,520
Sail.

87
00:03:52,060 --> 00:03:53,020
Listen to the vowels.

88
00:03:54,200 --> 00:03:54,380
Leaf...

89
00:03:55,540 --> 00:03:56,440
Feel...

90
00:03:56,960 --> 00:03:56,960
Tool...

91
00:03:57,040 --> 00:04:00,780
Those are long vowels.

92
00:04:01,540 --> 00:04:02,920
They say their own names.

93
00:04:04,080 --> 00:04:05,140
Now look at the endings.

94
00:04:06,180 --> 00:04:07,980
Only one F.

95
00:04:08,120 --> 00:04:09,940
Only one L.

96
00:04:09,940 --> 00:04:10,900
No doubling.

97
00:04:12,160 --> 00:04:13,620
The vowel decides.

98
00:04:14,200 --> 00:04:15,120
Short vowel...

99
00:04:15,600 --> 00:04:16,080
double it.

100
00:04:17,140 --> 00:04:18,020
Long vowel...

101
00:04:18,440 --> 00:04:18,720
don't.

102
00:04:19,860 --> 00:04:21,460
Always check the vowel first.

103
00:04:23,440 --> 00:04:25,130
There are three little traps.

104
00:04:25,680 --> 00:04:26,740
Trap number one.

105
00:04:27,560 --> 00:04:31,900
Only F, L, S and Z double.

106
00:04:32,500 --> 00:04:33,820
Not C.

107
00:04:33,820 --> 00:04:34,680
Not K.

108
00:04:35,280 --> 00:04:36,620
Not P.

109
00:04:36,620 --> 00:04:37,500
Not T.

110
00:04:38,940 --> 00:04:39,870
Trap number two.

111
00:04:40,600 --> 00:04:41,380
Long words.

112
00:04:42,320 --> 00:04:43,880
Listen.

113
00:04:43,940 --> 00:04:44,620
Travel.

114
00:04:46,160 --> 00:04:46,960
Clap it with me.

115
00:04:47,800 --> 00:04:48,440
Tra...

116
00:04:49,280 --> 00:04:50,000
vel.

117
00:04:51,060 --> 00:04:52,230
That's two claps.

118
00:04:53,000 --> 00:04:55,960
The Floss rule only works on one-clap words.

119
00:04:56,920 --> 00:04:59,000
So travel keeps one L.

120
00:05:00,100 --> 00:05:02,040
The same with pencil.

121
00:05:03,160 --> 00:05:03,480
Pen...

122
00:05:04,440 --> 00:05:05,160
cil.

123
00:05:06,200 --> 00:05:07,400
Two claps...

124
00:05:07,480 --> 00:05:08,280
one L.

125
00:05:09,640 --> 00:05:10,300
One clap...

126
00:05:10,780 --> 00:05:11,220
double it.

127
00:05:12,100 --> 00:05:13,430
More than one clap...

128
00:05:13,740 --> 00:05:14,540
leave it alone.

129
00:05:15,940 --> 00:05:17,320
Trap number three.

130
00:05:17,600 --> 00:05:18,580
Look at this word.

131
00:05:19,400 --> 00:05:19,900
Bee.

132
00:05:20,700 --> 00:05:24,660
It ends with two Es, but that's a vowel team.

133
00:05:25,180 --> 00:05:26,780
It's not the Floss rule.

134
00:05:27,620 --> 00:05:32,540
Floss only works with F, L, S and Z.

135
00:05:34,200 --> 00:05:35,980
Here's an easy way to remember it.

136
00:05:36,840 --> 00:05:40,020
The word Floss follows its own rule.

137
00:05:40,480 --> 00:05:41,000
One clap.

138
00:05:41,540 --> 00:05:42,580
A short o.

139
00:05:43,080 --> 00:05:44,220
And a double S.

140
00:05:45,320 --> 00:05:47,620
Floss tells you exactly what to do.

141
00:05:48,420 --> 00:05:49,520
Pretty clever, isn't it?

142
00:05:51,140 --> 00:05:52,700
Now for our second rule.

143
00:05:53,460 --> 00:05:55,660
This one is about the /k/ sound.

144
00:05:56,580 --> 00:05:57,290
Here's the problem.

145
00:05:58,340 --> 00:06:01,560
Three different spellings can make the same /k/ sound.

146
00:06:02,440 --> 00:06:03,280
C can make it.

147
00:06:04,220 --> 00:06:05,280
K can make it.

148
00:06:05,940 --> 00:06:08,260
And C-K can make it too.

149
00:06:08,940 --> 00:06:11,040
So how do we know which one to write?

150
00:06:12,160 --> 00:06:13,080
It's not a guess.

151
00:06:13,760 --> 00:06:17,140
There's a rule, and it depends on two things.

152
00:06:17,800 --> 00:06:20,060
Where the /k/ sound is in the word...

153
00:06:20,760 --> 00:06:22,380
and what letter comes next.

154
00:06:23,260 --> 00:06:25,280
Let's take them one at a time.

155
00:06:26,360 --> 00:06:27,800
First, the letter C.

156
00:06:28,860 --> 00:06:35,300
Use C at the start of a word when the next letter is A, O or U.

157
00:06:36,480 --> 00:06:36,920
Listen.

158
00:06:37,580 --> 00:06:37,800
Cat.

159
00:06:39,440 --> 00:06:42,960
The next letter is A, so we write C.

160
00:06:44,360 --> 00:06:44,460
Cup.

161
00:06:45,540 --> 00:06:48,880
The next letter is U, so we write C.

162
00:06:50,420 --> 00:06:51,220
Here are some more.

163
00:06:52,200 --> 00:06:52,680
Cod.

164
00:06:55,500 --> 00:06:56,140
Cot.

165
00:06:56,160 --> 00:06:57,400
Cab.

166
00:06:57,400 --> 00:06:57,860
Cut.

167
00:06:59,440 --> 00:07:05,820
Every one starts with C, and every one is followed by A, O or U.

168
00:07:06,900 --> 00:07:08,720
But there's one special rule.

169
00:07:09,600 --> 00:07:13,480
Before E and I, the letter C usually says /sss/.

170
00:07:15,320 --> 00:07:15,900
Listen.

171
00:07:16,720 --> 00:07:17,300
City.

172
00:07:18,020 --> 00:07:21,280
The C says /sss/, not /kuh/.

173
00:07:22,160 --> 00:07:26,320
So when we want the /k/ sound before E or I...

174
00:07:26,820 --> 00:07:28,580
we write K instead.

175
00:07:29,960 --> 00:07:30,240
Key.

176
00:07:31,120 --> 00:07:31,580
Kit.

177
00:07:32,420 --> 00:07:33,220
King.

178
00:07:34,240 --> 00:07:35,040
So remember...

179
00:07:35,660 --> 00:07:38,920
C before A, O and U.

180
00:07:39,720 --> 00:07:41,940
K before E and I.

181
00:07:44,000 --> 00:07:45,060
Now for the third part.

182
00:07:45,940 --> 00:07:48,870
This is the rule that gives C-K its name.

183
00:07:49,400 --> 00:07:52,500
When the /k/ sound comes at the end of a word...

184
00:07:53,120 --> 00:07:55,700
and it comes right after a short vowel...

185
00:07:56,260 --> 00:07:58,120
we write C-K.

186
00:07:59,300 --> 00:08:00,420
Listen.

187
00:08:00,480 --> 00:08:00,720
Duck.

188
00:08:02,480 --> 00:08:02,820
Back.

189
00:08:04,380 --> 00:08:04,680
Kick.

190
00:08:06,260 --> 00:08:06,620
Rock.

191
00:08:08,280 --> 00:08:08,540
Luck.

192
00:08:10,000 --> 00:08:10,460
Pack.

193
00:08:11,980 --> 00:08:12,540
Sock.

194
00:08:13,920 --> 00:08:14,400
Block.

195
00:08:15,860 --> 00:08:16,360
Check.

196
00:08:17,820 --> 00:08:18,120
Thick.

197
00:08:18,980 --> 00:08:21,360
Every one ends with C-K.

198
00:08:22,060 --> 00:08:24,640
And every one has a short vowel before it.

199
00:08:25,460 --> 00:08:26,430
That's one small rule...

200
00:08:26,880 --> 00:08:28,240
used in lots of words.

201
00:08:29,800 --> 00:08:31,440
Here's something really important.

202
00:08:32,420 --> 00:08:34,260
C-K is two letters...

203
00:08:34,780 --> 00:08:36,940
but it makes just one sound.

204
00:08:37,860 --> 00:08:39,160
Not /kuh/...

205
00:08:39,160 --> 00:08:39,260
/kuh/.

206
00:08:40,180 --> 00:08:41,360
Just one /k/ sound.

207
00:08:43,080 --> 00:08:43,420
Listen.

208
00:08:44,400 --> 00:08:44,620
Duck.

209
00:08:46,000 --> 00:08:46,680
Du...ck.

210
00:08:47,920 --> 00:08:49,520
One /k/ at the end.

211
00:08:50,240 --> 00:08:51,120
Two letters...

212
00:08:51,620 --> 00:08:52,080
One sound.

213
00:08:53,040 --> 00:08:54,800
That's all C-K means.

214
00:08:56,880 --> 00:08:58,880
Now let's look at some common mistakes.

215
00:09:00,060 --> 00:09:00,600
Duck...

216
00:09:00,660 --> 00:09:01,160
is correct.

217
00:09:02,460 --> 00:09:02,660
Dukk...

218
00:09:03,420 --> 00:09:04,240
is wrong.

219
00:09:05,280 --> 00:09:05,720
Ducc...

220
00:09:06,540 --> 00:09:08,700
is wrong.

221
00:09:08,780 --> 00:09:09,000
Duk...

222
00:09:09,740 --> 00:09:10,430
is wrong.

223
00:09:11,580 --> 00:09:13,620
English never doubles C.

224
00:09:14,440 --> 00:09:17,640
And it never doubles K for this sound.

225
00:09:18,020 --> 00:09:19,800
Don't swap them around either.

226
00:09:20,600 --> 00:09:21,560
Cat needs a C.

227
00:09:23,220 --> 00:09:25,000
Kat is not an English word.

228
00:09:25,700 --> 00:09:27,360
Here's an easy trick to remember.

229
00:09:28,380 --> 00:09:29,590
Short vowel at the end?

230
00:09:30,080 --> 00:09:30,940
Use C-K.

231
00:09:31,800 --> 00:09:32,420
Think of a duck.

232
00:09:33,500 --> 00:09:33,720
Duck...

233
00:09:34,840 --> 00:09:35,800
Quack!

234
00:09:36,820 --> 00:09:37,420
Say it once...

235
00:09:38,740 --> 00:09:40,270
and you'll remember it forever.

236
00:09:41,800 --> 00:09:43,560
Let's remember today's two rules.

237
00:09:45,040 --> 00:09:45,280
Floss.

238
00:09:46,680 --> 00:09:47,460
One syllable.

239
00:09:47,780 --> 00:09:48,660
A short vowel.

240
00:09:49,740 --> 00:09:54,100
Ending in F, L, S or Z.

241
00:09:54,120 --> 00:09:55,650
Double the last letter.

242
00:09:56,640 --> 00:09:57,860
And the C-K rule.

243
00:09:58,580 --> 00:10:00,900
C before A, O and U.

244
00:10:01,580 --> 00:10:03,440
K before E and I.

245
00:10:04,380 --> 00:10:06,920
C-K at the end after a short vowel.

246
00:10:08,180 --> 00:10:09,280
That's two rules finished!

247
00:10:09,820 --> 00:10:10,880
Great job today.

248
00:10:11,960 --> 00:10:18,880
Next time we'll learn N-G, N-K, X, and the tricky W.

249
00:10:18,880 --> 00:10:19,540
Don't miss it!

250
00:10:20,700 --> 00:10:24,480
If you enjoyed this lesson, please like this video and subscribe.

251
00:10:24,980 --> 00:10:27,520
And practise these rules in the English Learning app.

252
00:10:28,240 --> 00:10:31,020
It's free on the Apple App Store and Google Play.

253
00:10:31,500 --> 00:10:32,370
See you next time!
